---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import SubNav from '../components/SubNav.astro';
import SubFooter from '../components/SubFooter.astro';

const description =
  'Research notes from Now City: what the record of new-city attempts, precedent districts, and liveability outcomes actually teaches.';
---

<Layout title="Research Notes" description={description}>
  <SubNav current="/research" />

  <section class="pt-12 md:pt-16">
    <div class="container-narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">Research</p>
      <h1 class="mt-4 font-display text-4xl leading-tight text-forest-700 md:text-5xl">We do the homework, and we show it.</h1>
      <p class="lede mt-6">Notes from the reading, the site visits, and the record. Short, sourced where it matters, and honest about what we do not know yet.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article id="manifesto-wave" class="py-section">
    <div class="container-narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">Note 01 · August 2026</p>
      <h2 class="mt-3 font-display text-3xl text-forest-700">What the manifesto wave teaches</h2>
      <div class="prose-body mt-6">
        <p>A generation of founders has tried to publish the physical world into existence. Before writing our own manifesto, we studied every attempt we could find.</p>
        <p>Travis Kalanick built in stealth for eight years, published the Atoms vision this summer, and landed the largest check Andreessen Horowitz has ever written. Atoms is industrial AI rather than a city, but the lesson travels: the words worked because the proof came first. Marc Andreessen never built a place at all, yet "It's Time to Build" may be the most consequential essay of the decade; it named the problem so clearly that an investment category grew around it. California Forever assembled an extraordinary vision and extraordinary backers, and is learning in public that the hardest capital to raise is a community's trust; to their credit, they have stayed at the table. Telosa gave us beautiful renderings and a genuinely interesting land-trust model, and five years on is still searching for its ground. Praxis proved a manifesto alone can attract half a billion in commitments, and that commitments wait for a place to land.</p>
        <p>And Culdesac, with the humblest thesis of them all, built it: seventeen acres in Tempe, car-free, thriving, convincing enough that its most prominent critic publicly revised its judgment, and the next city over selected Culdesac for a downtown site it had struggled to develop for decades.</p>
        <p>The pattern is hard to miss. Vision pulls capital. Ground truth, a real site, real neighbors, a real clock, and someone who stays, is what makes vision compound. Our manifesto starts where the record says to start: with one neighborhood, on a riverfront in Oregon, where the work has already begun. <a href="/manifesto">Read it here.</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <article id="demand-gap" class="py-section bg-cream-50">
    <div class="container-narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">Note 02 · August 2026</p>
      <h2 class="mt-3 font-display text-3xl text-forest-700">The walkability gap is a supply problem</h2>
      <div class="prose-body mt-6">
        <p>Survey after survey finds a majority of Americans would choose a walkable neighborhood, and many would pay more for it. Yet walkable urbanism is a small fraction of what gets built, and the scarcity shows up as a price premium in nearly every market that has any.</p>
        <p>Read that premium carefully: it is the market saying <em>build more of this</em>, and the building industry answering <em>we cannot</em>. Parcel-by-parcel development struggles to deliver whole walkable places because the value sits between the parcels: in the street, the market hall, the park, the way homes and daily life share a block. Nobody who owns one parcel can build that alone.</p>
        <p>That is the gap our model is designed for: plan the neighborhood as one system, deliver it in phases, and let the value between the buildings pay its own way. The premium is not the point; closing the gap is. When supply catches up to what people already want, attainability and walkability stop being opposites.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <article id="district-lessons" class="py-section">
    <div class="container-narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">Note 03 · August 2026</p>
      <h2 class="mt-3 font-display text-3xl text-forest-700">What great districts teach</h2>
      <div class="prose-body mt-6">
        <p>We keep a study set of the neighborhood-scale transformations that actually worked: Portland's Pearl District, Copenhagen's harbor neighborhoods, Stockholm's Hammarby Sjöstad, Vancouver's False Creek, and a handful of others, each visited or researched in depth.</p>
        <p>Three lessons repeat. First, the early structural moves do most of the work: the street grid, the transit decision, the public realm commitments made before the first building. Second, coherence and variety are not enemies; the best districts were built by many hands inside one clear plan, which is why they feel alive instead of stamped. Third, value compounds for decades in these places, and most of it lands after the original developers have left. The role almost nobody plays is the steward who stays, which is why staying is one of our founding commitments.</p>
        <p>The record is generous with its lessons. It mostly asks that someone do the reading.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <article id="outcomes" class="py-section bg-cream-50">
    <div class="container-narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">Note 04 · August 2026</p>
      <h2 class="mt-3 font-display text-3xl text-forest-700">Six outcomes that compound</h2>
      <div class="prose-body mt-6">
        <p>Walkable, mixed-use, regenerative neighborhoods perform better on the measures that matter to the people who live in them and the cities that host them: mobility (fewer miles behind a windshield, more of life on foot), carbon (lower to build, lower to run), health (daily movement, cleaner air, more green, more connection), local economy (small businesses that survive, spending that stays), resilience (district systems that keep working when the grid has a bad week), and long-term value (walkable places hold and grow value, and the premium tends to widen as the neighborhood matures).</p>
        <p>The research base for each of these is deep, and our commitment is to measure our own performance against it in the open: real numbers from a real place, published as the neighborhood grows. Places should have to show their work too.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <SubFooter />
</Layout>
